.\" Man page for nic-report.py
.\" Written in classic troff/groff format
.TH NIC-REPORT 8 "2026-10-26" "nic-report 2.13.0" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
nic-report \- single- and multi-site NIC health reports from nic-sweep sweeps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nic-report.py
.RI [ SWEEP_DIR ...]
.RI [ \-R | \-\-sweep\-root
.IR DIR ]
.RI [ \-s | \-\-site
.IR SITE ]...
.RI [ \-a | \-\-at
.IR DATE ]
.RI [ \-i | \-\-import
.I SITE PATH
.RI ...]...
.RI [ \-r | \-\-role\-regex
.IR RE ]
.RI [ \-O | \-\-output\-format
.IR md | json ]
.RI [ \-o | \-\-output
.IR FILE ]
.RI [ \-t | \-\-title
.IR TEXT ]
.RI [ \-c | \-\-classification
.IR TEXT ]
.RI [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.RI [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nic-report.py
reads the sweeps written by
.BR nic-sweep.sh (8)
(and, for older collections, nic-health CSV files) and writes a report for one
site, several sites, or every site of a sweep tree. The report has three
sections:
.TP
.B Coverage and comparability
Hosts, roles, NIC ASICs and ports, ports with link, deep checks evaluated, FEC
modes, nic-health versions, sampling windows and collection dates; every host
that was not collected; and caveats such as mixed tool versions, imported data
without collection metadata, or sites with no sweep at the requested date.
.TP
.B Verdicts
Hosts by verdict and the share of clean hosts, per site and per role.
.TP
.B Findings
Every WARN, CRIT and UNKNOWN result, worst first, grouped by site and host, with
its evidence and hint.
.PP
With one site the coverage table has a column per role and the verdicts list
each host; with several sites the columns are sites. The tool is read-only and
offline: it never contacts a host or the network.
.PP
It is the one Python script of the nic-xray tools (Python 3.9 or newer,
standard library only).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I SWEEP_DIR
A sweep directory
.RI ( DIR / site / YYYYmmddTHHMMSSZ )
to report on as is.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-sweep\-root " " \fIDIR\fR
A nic-sweep tree. Selects the latest sweep of each site (or of each
.BR \-\-site ).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-site " " \fISITE\fR
Only this site of the tree. Repeatable. An unknown site is an error that lists
the sites available.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-at " " \fIDATE\fR
Per site, the latest sweep on or before
.I DATE
(UTC):
.B YYYY-MM-DD
(end of that day) or
.BR YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM[:SS]Z .
Sites without such a sweep are listed as missing in the report.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-import " " "\fISITE PATH\fR ..."
Legacy nic-health CSV for
.IR SITE :
files, or directories of
.B *.csv
files. One file per host (host name taken from the file name up to the first
.B _
or
.BR . ),
or merged files with
.B site
and
.B hostname
columns. CRLF line endings, leading blank lines and fields wrapped by a terminal
capture are repaired. Imported data has no collection metadata, which the
report notes. A host may come from one file only, and a file that adds no row
for
.I SITE
is an error. Repeatable.
.B \-\-import
takes every following path: put
.I SWEEP_DIR
arguments before it, or end its list with
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-role\-regex " " \fIRE\fR
Derive each host's role from its name: the group named
.B role
(or the first group; a regex without a group is a usage error). Without it,
roles are empty.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-output\-format " " \fImd\fR|\fIjson\fR
Markdown (default) or JSON with every computed value.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write to
.I FILE
instead of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-title " " \fITEXT\fR
Report title (default: "NIC Health: SITE" or "NIC Health: cross-site
comparison (...)").
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-classification " " \fITEXT\fR
Classification label printed on the report.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help and exit.
.PP
A site may appear only once in a selection: a sweep directory and the same
site from
.B \-\-sweep\-root
is an error. Under
.BR \-\-sweep\-root ,
a sweep whose
.B sweep.json
names another site than its directory is an error. Symbolic links in the tree
are followed. Long options cannot be abbreviated.
.SH NOTES
A port counts as having link when nic-health reported its link state as
.BR up .
nic-health older than 2.12.0 records no link state: ports are then counted from
the link-flap records and ports with link are shown as unknown, with a caveat.
A collected host whose
.BI nodes/ host .json
is missing or malformed is listed with the hosts not collected (status
.BR unreadable-result )
instead of failing the report. The clean share is the share of collected hosts
whose verdict is OK.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Report written.
.TP
.B 1
Input error: unreadable input, an unsupported sweep schema, an unknown site, a
site selected twice, no data for the selection (for example
.B \-\-at
before every sweep); or the output file cannot be written.
.TP
.B 3
Usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Latest sweep of every site, roles from host names like prd-site-ctrl2d-01:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nic-report.py \-R sweeps \-\-role\-regex '^[^\-]+\-[^\-]+\-(?P<role>[a\-z]+\\d+[a\-z]*)\-\\d+'
.fi
.RE
.PP
One site as it was on a date, as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-report.py \-R sweeps \-s SITE1 \-a 2026-10-23 \-O json \-o site1.json
.RE
.PP
Mix a sweep tree with an older hand-made collection:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-report.py \-R sweeps \-i SITE9 old/SITE9/
.RE
